#[
  #set text(size: 9.0pt)
  #set par(leading: 0.63em, spacing: 0.68em)

  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Usman dan Fodio founded the caliphate after the jihad; his son Muhammad Bello built Sokoto as its capital, Rumfa was a pre-jihad king of Kano, and al-Kanemi ruled Borno outside the caliphate.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emir held his emirate from the caliph, who could appoint and depose him. The emirate council advised and could restrain, but appointment and deposition belonged to the caliph.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The council brought together the senior officials and title holders of the emirate. It advised the emir on policy and acted as a check on him; it was not elected.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Alkali were the judges of the Sharia courts, handling civil and criminal matters under Islamic law. Customary and English law belong to other systems and later periods.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emirate was divided into districts under district heads, and the districts into villages under village heads. The council of elders and the age grades belong to the Igbo system.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The council of senior officials and the caliph's power of deposition together limited the emir. He was not an absolute ruler, and the paper often asks for that check.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oba ruled with a council of chiefs who could restrain him — a centralised monarchy, but not an absolute one. The Igbo system is the stateless one.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oyomesi were the seven senior chiefs of Oyo, and the Bashorun was their head. They could reject the Alaafin, which is the classic Yoruba check.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Bashorun presided over the Oyomesi. The Alaafin was the king, the Are-Ona-Kakanfo the commander of the army, and Oba the generic title of a Yoruba ruler.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Are-Ona-Kakanfo commanded the Oyo army and was expected to win or die in battle. He was not a member of the Oyomesi, which is a favourite distinction in the paper.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Iyalode was the leading woman of the town, with a seat among the chiefs and responsibility for women's interests. The Ozo is Igbo and the Alkali is Hausa–Fulani.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    “Acephalous” means without a single head. Igbo communities governed themselves through councils of elders, age grades, title societies and assemblies rather than through a king.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The elders held the ofo, the symbol of truth and ancestral authority, and their influence rested on age and wisdom rather than on an office created from above.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Ozo title was bought and conferred prestige; title holders sat among those whose opinion weighed in the assembly. It was not a military or a tax office.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Age grades grouped people of the same generation for work, defence and discipline. The other three institutions belong to the Hausa–Fulani and Yoruba systems.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Ibini Ukpabi oracle at Arochukwu was consulted in disputes and its verdicts carried authority across Igboland — one of the few institutions with an inter-community reach.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The assembly debated until consensus emerged; voting by count and royal decrees belong to other systems. The oracle could be consulted, but it did not govern.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The absence of a single ruler is the defining difference. The emirate was hierarchical and the Igbo system segmentary, which is exactly why indirect rule worked in one and failed in the other.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both had a ruler and a council: the Alaafin with the Oyomesi, the emir with the emirate council. Both also had courts, which rules out the fourth option.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emirate collected tribute from districts, levied taxes such as the zakka and jizya, and taxed trade routes. Salary tax and oil revenue belong to the modern state.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The desert routes carried salt, horses and goods north and south, and the states taxed the trade. It supplemented agriculture rather than replacing it.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The caliph at Sokoto was the supreme authority, and the emirs held their emirates under him with a large measure of local autonomy — a federal arrangement in substance.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A hereditary emir belongs to the Hausa–Fulani system. The other three are Igbo institutions, and their presence together is what the paper expects.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oyomesi selected the new Oba from the ruling house, subject to the appropriate rites. It was neither a popular election nor a British appointment before 1900.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oba in council heard cases, with chiefs and the Iyalode involved in different matters. The Alkali court is Islamic and belongs to the north.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The colonial administration grafted itself onto existing institutions — emirs, Obas, elders — and the shape of that graft explains the successes and failures of indirect rule in Chapter 2.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The caliph appointed the emir, advised him and could depose him. There was no written constitution or parliament in the modern sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oyomesi could present the Alaafin with an empty calabash, the signal that he should die. It is the most dramatic check in the pre-colonial systems and a favourite examination example.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The village square or market place was the forum; the emir's and Oba's palaces belong to the other two systems.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The variety is the point: emirates, Yoruba kingdoms and Igbo village republics. A question that says “all” is usually wrong in this topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Iyalode represented women's interests among the chiefs. The Bashorun headed the Oyomesi, the Alkali judged under Sharia, and the Ozo was an Igbo title.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Alkali decided cases under Sharia, and appeals went to the emir and beyond him to the caliph. The other institutions belong to the Yoruba or Igbo systems.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The district head answered to the emir, who answered to the caliph. The British Resident is a colonial officer and belongs to Chapter 2.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    “Stateless” describes the absence of a centralised state apparatus, not the absence of law or government. The Igbo had courts, rules and authority — dispersed among institutions.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Capture in war and raids supplied the slave trade, both trans-Saharan and Atlantic. The other options have no connection to the supply of slaves.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The elders did not command a centralised army; defence was organised through age grades and village bodies. The other three were ordinary functions of the council.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Indirect rule needed a chief to rule through, and the emirate supplied one at every level. Where no single ruler existed, the British had to invent one — the warrant chief — with poor results.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The checks — the council, the Oyomesi, the assembly — are the local ancestors of the separation of powers, and the paper sometimes asks for that continuity.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emir appointed the village head, usually from the family that custom recognised. The caliph dealt with emirs, not villages.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Igbo system lacked a single hereditary ruler, which is what distinguishes it from the emirate and the Yoruba kingdom. The other statements are true.
  ]

]

